================================================================================
FEATURE 6: LOCAL STORAGE
================================================================================

An important challenge with persisted items is:

Date objects do not remain Date objects after JSON serialization.


Example before saving:

createdAt:
new Date()


After:

JSON.stringify(...)

and:

JSON.parse(...)


createdAt becomes a string.


Therefore this can fail after reloading persisted data:

item.createdAt
  .toLocaleTimeString()


because the parsed value is no longer a Date object.


ONE APPROACH:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Store an ISO string intentionally:

createdAt:
new Date().toISOString()


When displaying:

new Date(
  item.createdAt
).toLocaleTimeString()


================================================================================
LOCAL STORAGE - SAFER INITIALIZATION PATTERN
================================================================================

const [items, setItems] =
  useState(() => {

    const saved =
      localStorage.getItem(
        'items'
      )


    if (!saved) {

      return []
    }


    try {

      return JSON.parse(saved)

    } catch {

      return []
    }
  })


useEffect(() => {

  localStorage.setItem(
    'items',
    JSON.stringify(items)
  )

}, [items])


================================================================================
IMPORTANT LOCAL STORAGE NOTE
================================================================================

This keeps data in the user's browser.

It is not equivalent to:

• Backend persistence

• Multi-device synchronization

• Shared application storage

• A database


================================================================================
FEATURE 7: EXPORT
================================================================================

A client-side JSON export conceptually involves:

items
  ↓
JSON.stringify()
  ↓
Blob
  ↓
Temporary Object URL
  ↓
Download


If an object URL is created:

const url =
  URL.createObjectURL(blob)


clean it up afterward:

URL.revokeObjectURL(url)


This avoids leaving unnecessary temporary object URLs allocated.


